# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Agorasim web — environment variables
# Copy to `.env.local` for local development. Never commit real secrets.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Neon Postgres connection string (pooled URL from the Neon dashboard).
# Used by Drizzle at runtime (server actions / server components) and by
# `drizzle-kit migrate` in the GitHub "DB migrate" workflow.
# Example: postgresql://user:password@ep-xxx-pooler.eu-central-1.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require
DATABASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Admin area (see src/lib/admin-session.ts and src/lib/admin-auth.ts)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The HMAC key that signs admin session cookies. REQUIRED in every environment,
# including local development — there is no built-in fallback, so a misconfigured
# deployment fails closed rather than minting sessions with a public secret.
# Rotating it invalidates every live session.
#
#   cd web
#   printf 'ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=%s\n' "$(openssl rand -base64 32)" >> .env.local
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=

# The first owner account, used by `pnpm db:seed-owner` so a fresh deployment is
# never locked out of its own admin. Seeding is idempotent: if the address
# already has an account it is left completely alone, password included.
#
#   cd web
#   ADMIN_SEED_EMAIL=diogo@agorasim.pt ADMIN_SEED_NAME=Diogo \
#   ADMIN_SEED_PASSWORD='a long passphrase' pnpm db:seed-owner
#
# The password must be at least 12 characters (src/lib/password-policy.ts) and
# should be changed from /admin/settings/account on first sign-in.
#
# These are the *only* route to a first account. The login form authenticates
# against `admin_users` and nothing else — the old shared `ADMIN_PASSWORD` is
# gone, so a deployment with an empty table and no seed secrets has no way in
# and has to be seeded from a database console.
ADMIN_SEED_EMAIL=
ADMIN_SEED_NAME=
ADMIN_SEED_PASSWORD=

# Break-glass password reset for an operator locked out of /admin, read by
# `pnpm db:reset-password`. Unlike the seed above it only ever updates an
# account that already exists, and no deploy step runs it.
#
#   cd web
#   ADMIN_RESET_EMAIL=diogo@agorasim.pt \
#   ADMIN_RESET_PASSWORD='a long passphrase' pnpm db:reset-password
#
# Pass these on the command line rather than storing them here — a reset
# password sitting in .env.local outlives the reason it was needed.
ADMIN_RESET_EMAIL=
ADMIN_RESET_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data retention (see src/lib/retention.ts and docs/data-protection.md)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# How long an enquiry that never converted is kept before the scheduled job
# anonymises it. Whole days.
#
# ⚠️ 730 (24 months) is the fallback if unset, and it is a PROPOSAL that nobody
# has signed off. Set this explicitly once the period has actually been decided.
ENQUIRY_RETENTION_DAYS=730

# How long an audit entry keeps the IP address it was recorded from, before the
# same job nulls it. Whole days. The entry itself is kept — only this one field
# expires.
#
# 90 is the fallback, and unlike the period above it is a recommendation rather
# than a placeholder: the usual window for security logs, long enough to
# investigate an incident somebody noticed late, short enough that the column is
# not a standing record of where the team works from.
AUDIT_IP_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# Shared secret for the scheduled retention route (/api/cron/retention). Vercel
# Cron sends it as `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`. Without it the route
# refuses every request rather than running open — it deletes personal data, so
# an unauthenticated version of it would be a weapon pointed at the sales
# pipeline.
#
#   printf 'CRON_SECRET=%s\n' "$(openssl rand -base64 32)" >> .env.local
CRON_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Third parties
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Vercel Blob, where experience photos uploaded from /admin/experiences are
# stored. On Vercel the token appears automatically once a Blob store is
# connected to the project (Storage → Create → Blob); for local development,
# copy it from the store's settings.
#
# When unset, the rest of the catalogue editor keeps working — only uploads
# fail, with an inline message telling the operator storage isn't configured.
# Rows keep whatever image they already have.
BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Payments — Stripe Checkout (see src/lib/stripe.ts)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The secret key. **Leaving this unset is a supported state**, not a broken one:
# `/reservar` then offers the enquiry form instead of a payment, which is
# exactly the launch plan's fallback if Stripe activation slips (Risks table).
# Flip payments on by setting the key — no deploy branch, no revert.
#
# A `sk_test_…` key puts the checkout screen into a labelled test mode, so a
# rehearsal on a phone cannot be mistaken for a real payment.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# Signing secret for the webhook at /api/stripe/webhook — the endpoint that
# actually confirms a booking. Without it the route refuses every request: an
# unverified endpoint that marks bookings paid is worse than no endpoint.
#
# From the Stripe dashboard (Developers → Webhooks → your endpoint), or from
# `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/stripe/webhook` locally.
#
# Subscribe the endpoint to: checkout.session.completed,
# checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded, checkout.session.async_payment_failed,
# checkout.session.expired.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The client's own Stripe account, linked to the platform through Stripe Connect
# (see src/lib/stripe.ts). Set it and the checkout session, its payment intent and
# its charge are all created *on that account* — a Stripe direct charge — which is
# what the commission agreement describes: the guest pays Agorasim, Agorasim is the
# merchant of record and bears Stripe's processing fee, and the platform is paid an
# application fee out of the payment rather than by invoice.
#
# **Leaving this unset is a supported state** and the current one: the client's
# account does not exist yet, so every charge is a plain platform charge exactly as
# before. Nothing else has to change to switch — no fee is taken either way yet.
#
# An `acct_…` id, not a key: it identifies an account, it does not authorise
# anything, and the authorisation is the platform's own STRIPE_SECRET_KEY above.
# A value that is not an `acct_…` id makes the app refuse payments rather than
# charge them to the wrong account.
#
# When it is set, point STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET at a webhook endpoint that listens to
# events *on connected accounts* — direct-charge events arrive there, carrying the
# account id, and the route drops any that came from a different account.
STRIPE_CONNECTED_ACCOUNT_ID=

# Where this deployment lives. Drives two things (see src/lib/site-origin.ts):
# the origin Stripe sends the guest back to and email assets resolve against,
# and the canonical address — every <link rel="canonical">, hreflang, sitemap
# URL, robots.txt host and JSON-LD @id (`site.domain`). Production sets it to
# the live domain, so switch day is this one value flipped to
# https://agorasim.pt plus a redeploy.
#
# Unset, the two answers part ways on purpose. Canonicals fall back to
# https://agorasim.pt — never the Vercel URL, so a preview is not indexed as the
# real page — while Stripe returns and email links follow the Vercel-provided
# URL on a preview (so a preview's checkout returns to that preview) and
# http://localhost:3000 in local development.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Transactional email — Resend (see src/lib/email.ts)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# API key. Unset means confirmation emails are skipped and logged; a booking is
# still taken and still lands on the Sales board, which is where the team would
# look anyway.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# The From: address. Must be on a domain verified in Resend or the API refuses
# the send. Example: Agorasim <reservas@agorasim.pt>
BOOKING_EMAIL_FROM=Agorasim <reservas@agorasim.pt>

# Who gets the team's copy of a new paid booking. Comma-separated — there are
# two of them and there will be a third. Unset means nobody is emailed, which
# is deliberate: mailing bookings to an inbox nobody reads is worse than not
# mailing them, because it looks handled.
BOOKING_NOTIFICATION_EMAILS=info@agorasim.pt

# Signs the per-booking cancellation tokens the guest self-serve cancel link
# carries (src/lib/cancellation-token.ts). Required wherever bookings are taken;
# a booking created without it simply gets no cancel link. Rotating it
# invalidates every live cancel link at once. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
BOOKING_TOKEN_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Error tracking — Sentry (see src/lib/observability.ts and src/instrumentation.ts)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The project's DSN. **Unset means error tracking is off**: the SDK is never
# initialised and every capture is a no-op, so a local checkout or a preview
# without it behaves exactly as before — nothing throws, nothing is skipped.
#
# Server-side only. No Sentry code ships to the browser, so there is no client
# DSN to expose and the Content-Security-Policy is untouched. The
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_` name is honoured as a fallback only because it is the one the
# Vercel ↔ Sentry integration writes; prefer SENTRY_DSN, which stays private.
#
# What leaves the server: the error, the failing operation's tags, and — for an
# unhandled request error — the method, path and headers minus cookies and
# authorization. `sendDefaultPii` is off, so no IP addresses and no user data.
SENTRY_DSN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=

